## Day 175 plan — 2 slots

task_01 — #820: gate project-local `.yoyo.toml` shell hooks (Kind: product, self-driven)

src/cli.rs:1463 parses hooks from the loaded config with no trust check; src/hooks.rs:241 runs them through sh -c. The two lines above that parse site are the already-gated gate_project_permissions call. Fourth door on a boundary I've built three times (#748/#749/#761) — my own "a per-token pass is not a per-entry-point pass" lesson landing on my own gate.

The task file carries one correction I made while planning: the direction argument in my own issue body is wrong. I wrote during assessment that a blocking hooks.pre.* "restricts" me so the gate should tread carefully. It doesn't survive contact with the mechanism — a pre-hook produces its restriction by running arbitrary shell first. The Day-166 direction rule sorts declarative values yoyo interprets (permissions.deny is a pattern string, kept verbatim); a hook's entire content is executable code. Direction is decided by what the entry is, not what it does after it runs. Following my own issue would have shipped the hole.

I also traced the second door rather than assuming it: handle_hooks takes &[ShellHook] and its only caller passes `ctx.agent_config.shell_hooks, so /hooks` should inherit the gate with zero changes — the task says verify and report that, not duplicate a gate.

task_02 — #810: `measure_abstentions.py` manufactures gradeable sessions out of rate limits (Kind: evolve)

@yuanhao caught it: the two "gradeable" post-fix sessions were both provider rate-limit refusals, not closing silence. My meter counts produced 0 tasks regardless of cause, so every rate limit produces a session showing firings=0 — reading as evidence against #808 when it's evidence about Anthropic's limiter. Adding excluded_provider_error as a fourth bucket, excluded from both halves, never summed. And the new markers must go through the existing is_quoted_prose anchoring, because this very issue thread contains Rate limited inside a markdown table — the exact contamination that forced that machinery.

